WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.060
Good morning, John.

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:02.060
I very much enjoy making things.

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:05.850
And I think that most people enjoy making
things- making songs, making scarves, making

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:10.320
video blogs, making books, making it rain
like Roger...

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.170
People that like, take things that are inside
of their head and make them real in the real

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:14.170
world.

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:17.390
And this is hard, but I've been doing it for
a while now and I'm getting better at it.

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:21.550
I think that's because of a combination of
attitudes and resources that I now have.

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:24.029
And for other people who want to make things
and are maybe have a hard time doing it, I

00:00:24.029 --> 00:00:28.170
wanted to share my process in the form of
a case study: readit1st.com, which I launched

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:29.170
last night.

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:32.250
I had the idea over a year ago, and the process
of having and idea is very "plllblkdsj."

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:33.250
That just has to happen.

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:36.160
I liked the idea though, because it had a
lot of the things that I need for good ideas.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.870
One: It promotes something that I love: reading.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:41.860
Two: Through advertising, it could potentially
actually employ a writer.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:45.160
And I would love to be responsible for a writer
having a job.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.020
Three: It seemed kind of Nerdfightastic.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:49.430
It seemed kinda like something that Nerdfighters
would enjoy.

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:52.531
But first, before nerdfighteria, I had to
explain the idea to Katherine.

00:00:52.531 --> 00:00:56.960
Katherine is my wife and she is also my number
one wall at which to throw ideas, and if they

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:58.920
stick to her, then this is a very good sign.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.210
She's smart enough and loving enough to be
both quick and entirely merciless.

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:04.269
Now we have approached the most interesting
part of the process for me.

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:07.990
I took my initial feedback and I put it in
my head with the idea and sat there.

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:10.759
During the process, you have lots of great
ideas about the idea and how to make the idea

00:01:10.759 --> 00:01:11.759
perfect.

00:01:11.759 --> 00:01:14.280
What's not happening is the idea is not actually
becoming real.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.490
It's just becoming cooler inside of your brain.

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:20.619
We call this brain crack, and Ze Frank has
freed me from my addiction to it.

00:01:20.619 --> 00:01:24.520
So, I brain-cracked this idea for like a year,
and then I went to LeakyCon, where I got re-inspired

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:27.469
about books and adaptations and how people
should read the book first.

00:01:27.469 --> 00:01:31.450
I needed a domain name, went to bustaname.com,
begrudgingly settled on a domain name that

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:33.909
had a number in it, and this may not have
been the best decision in the world, but I

00:01:33.909 --> 00:01:35.840
want to emphasize that it WAS a decision.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:42.049
Making an unperfect decision is far, far better
than not making a decision, which is the worst

00:01:42.049 --> 00:01:43.310
possible decision you could make.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:47.392
Then I went into my design software, Fireworks,
and I made up a logo and I tweeted it and

00:01:47.392 --> 00:01:48.392
I put it on my tumblr.

00:01:48.392 --> 00:01:52.740
And lots of people gave me great feedback
and tons of amazing genius ideas about how

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:53.920
to make readit1st better.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:55.609
And I ignored all of them.

00:01:55.609 --> 00:01:58.899
Readit1st needs one thing: to do its job,
it needs a newsletter.

00:01:58.899 --> 00:02:00.999
And so that's all we did to start.

00:02:00.999 --> 00:02:06.060
We also went through about a dozen logo incarnations
on tumblr which was a really rewarding critique

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:07.060
for me.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:10.560
And then I, executive decision, settled on
a logo which we are now stuck with-- not because

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.250
it was the best logo possible, but because
we need to move on!

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:16.280
Then I designed a website around the logo
all by myself.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:18.260
I did this because I'm a passable designer.

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:22.310
And then because I'm no longer a passable
html coder, I sent it off to a company called

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:25.709
psd2html which changed my design into an actual
webpage.

00:02:25.709 --> 00:02:31.110
Then I suckered a friend of mine, Sam Rudge,
into making the code that makes the whole

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:32.110
site work.

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:36.930
And then I suckered my brother, John Green
into, uh, writing the write few editions of

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:37.930
the readit1st newsletter.

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:40.069
The first one will be going out Friday morning.

00:02:40.069 --> 00:02:43.120
Never worry about asking people to do favors
for you, especially when they're not huge,

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:45.180
gigantic hunks of time.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:46.180
BUT!

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:50.970
If money starts to exist with a project, always
remember those people who helped you and reward

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:51.970
them for that.

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:52.970
With money.

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:53.970
Do I need to say that?

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:54.970
Reward them with money!

00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:56.180
I do not know if this is a good idea.

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:57.180
That's the thing about ideas.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:00.790
You will never know if it's a good idea until
you make it real!

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:04.000
And when the world doesn't like the idea,
that is called failure.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.040
And it sounds and it feels SO much worse than
it is!

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:10.261
I now have a lot of failures under my belt,
and I don't mind so much anymore.

00:03:10.261 --> 00:03:12.230
It's an amazing way to feel if you want to
create things.

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:15.650
I mean, obviously success is much more enjoyable,
but if you're so afraid of the failure that

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:17.549
you never do it, your chances of success are
NONE.

00:03:17.549 --> 00:03:20.610
And of course I want all of Nerdfighteria
to sign up for readit1st, but I also want

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:23.140
you to know that this isn't something I created
for Nerdfighters.

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:24.650
I created it for the world.

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:27.400
What I did was I created it WITH Nerdfighters.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:28.760
And I really believe that.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:32.620
I really believe I couldn't have done any
of this if I didn't have a resource that no

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:35.910
one else in the world has-- except for John
Green.

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:43.400
A group of intelligent, interesting, interested,
savvy, tumblogging, made-of-awesome people.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:47.390
Thanks, not just for this, but for everything
that we've created together.

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:50.540
Everyone can go and sign up for readit1st.com
now, and don't forget to tell your friends.

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:51.840
John, I will see you on Friday.

